Step 3: Wiring
Power rules

SG90 servos need 5V power
ESP32 logic is 3.3V
The #1 rule: all grounds must be common
Wiring

Servo power

Servo red --> 5V supply
Servo brown/black --> GND supply
Servo signals ESP32-WROOM

Pan signal --> GPIO 25
Tilt signal --> GPIO 26
ESP32-CAM

Powered via the MB board or 5V input
Used for streaming video + optional detection


Step 4: Code a - ESP32-CAM
What this code does

Connects the ESP32-CAM to your Wifi.
Starts a simple MJPEG video stream you can open in any browser.
Open with "http://<your ip address>"
Captures frames at QQVGA (160×120) for maximum speed.
Runs face detection a few times per second.
Computes the face center error:
ex = faceX − centerX
ey = faceY − centerY
Applies a small deadband to reduce jitter (ignores tiny motion).
Sends ex/ey/found to the turret over ESP-NOW at a steady update rate.
ESP32-CAM code (Upload using Espressif ESP32 board manager version 1.0.6)

Board Name: AI Thinker ESP32-CAM.
Edits to make: ssid, password, MAC address.

Step 5: Code B: ESP32 WROOM
What this code does:

Connects the ESP32-WROOM to the same wifi as the ESP32-CAM.
Attaches two SG90 servos using hardware.
Listens for ESP-NOW packets containing ex/ey/found from the ESP32-CAM.
Uses a proportional control loop:
panTarget = pan − Kp*ex (mirrored, because you’re facing each other)
tiltTarget = tilt − Kp*ey (can toggle if inverted)
Applies smoothing so motion looks robotic, not twitchy.
ESP32-WROOM code (Upload using Espressif ESP32 board manager version 3.x)

Board Name: your ESP32-WROOM dev board
Important: Servos must have 5V power and a common ground with the ESP32.
Edits to make: ssid, password.


